DA sh*t canned? Best thing that could've happend...

Ok, I dont  know what happened with DA.  Did he quit, get fired, demoted, who knows.  
What I do know (who am I kidding, im speculating) is that this is the best thing that could
ever have happened to Horizons.

We all know that Horizons needed funding to continue and at the final hour, they did manage to 
get someone(s) with deep pockets to fund the continued development of the game.  But at what price?

If i'm Mr. Deep Pockets, there's no way I'm going to hand over all this cash and allow an unproven
company to do whatever the hell they feel like doing.  I'm going to require (in writing) a large
degree of control over the company... perhaps even demanding they he/she/they be the largest share 
holders. (they are putting up all the risk afterall!)  So in large respect, DA had to sign his (and the company's) 
soul away to Mr. Deep Pockets to keep his game alive.

Fast forward 4 to 6 months and money is still getting burned up at an alarming pace.  There's still 
nothing even remotely playable and the company is still trying to hire people with experience in the 
key areas of client/server development and large scale / high performance database programming.  

To all the new hires dismay, there are tons of design documents, but ZERO technical documents and all
of these have to be written from scrath.  Its one thing to say "we're going to have X,Y and Z" but its
another thing to figure out how to implement it programmatically.  

The clock is still flying several months later and still nothing remotely playable.  At this point, key
persons figure they are 12 months away from having a stripped down playable alpha and about 24 months
from a comparable game like Asheron's Call or Everquest and about 36 months from implementing DA's complete 
and unabridged dream feature list.  

Mr. Deep Pocket's is deeply concerned.  He consults privately with other members of the dev team (excluding DA)
and everyone becomes worried.  2 to 3 years from now they will have a ton of competition.  Their graphics
may be decidedly outdated before they ship. Asheron's Call and Everquest are not sitting still, they're liable to 
have killer new graphics by that time along with new features... potentially features that Horizons want to implement... 
meanwhile those games continue to eat up market share and tighten their grip on the consumer base.  

Reasonable minds prevail and conclude, we must scale back our design in order to get our game out in a reasonable 
time period.  Does it really make sense to throw all your eggs in one basket and hope that they are able to create the
definitive MMORG in 2 to 3 years time?  Or do they scale back, get a version out ASAP and start to build up a user base from
which they can then build on with future versions?  They consult DA and tell him they have serious doubts about whether some of 
these lofty design goals can be implemented in a reasonable time frame.  DA is probably accomodating here and agrees that some things
need to be scaled back for "Version 1.0."  Everyone in the company gets into the "Version 1.0" mindset (which happens
with every game at every company btw).  

Everyone is a bit more optimisitc but still, program development continues to falter.  Rifts start to form between
the developers.  Some developers complain that other developers arent pulling their weight.  Developer A needs 
module B completed by Developer C before he can really get started with module D.  Everyone starts to lose focus 
and the developers find themselves in more meetings than in actual development at their PC's (or at least it feels that way).
The feature set may have been scaled back, but sadly, none of the _real_ and serious problems with/within the project were
addressed.  

The optimism quickly fades back to pessimism.  The tools developer still hasnt completed some very significant utilities
which other developers have been waiting too  long for already.  The interface developer just isnt working out.  Some of the 
other "newbie" developers just aren't cutting it."  The dev lead is growing increasingly impatient and starts to realize that
the current team (at least in part) are incapable of fleshing out their areas of responsibility.  He thought they 
could get by with less than complete technical documentation, but many of the programmers are just not able to work
that autonmously... they need too much guidance... too much hand holding.  The whole project seems to be going nowhere fast.

Mr. Deep Pockets consults with Mr Dev Lead and says, "What do we need to do to get this project moving forward?"  Mr Dev Lead says,
"You need to give me more control so that I can get rid of some people, and bring on some more experienced developers." Mr. Deep Pockets
thinks about it for about half a second and agrees.  Mr. Deep Pockets informs DA that he's no longer the Project Lead.  DA
is pissed that everyone's been talking about things  of this nature behind his back.  He decides that he can no longer work there
as a second in command (a figure head really).  He figures FUCK'EM ALL and packs  his bags.  He's still got his shares of
stock in the company and he was certainly paid a salary for his position and figures if the game gets off the ground without him
and becomes a huge success, he'll still get paid in the end through his shares in the company.

Fast forward 2 years.  Horizons is in public beta.  The graphics are nice, but the game scale and scope is not significantly better
than the Asheron's Call or Everquest of 2000.  The game never catches on... there's at least 6 other MMORG's out (in addition to 2003/4 
versions of AC and EQ which put Horizons to shame) and everyone is competing for the same customer dollars.  Horizons shuts down
its servers 9 months after release to the dismay of a 2-3 thousand users.  

The End.

   
Well, at least with DA no longer at the helm, this project has a chance in hell at getting released.  Putting a dreamer like DA in
charge of such a technically challenging project is a recipe for disaster.  Show me a famous/successful game designer, and I'll show
you a great C/C++ programmer.  Ok, there are a few exceptions to this rule, but as time goes on, the number of NEW exceptions to this
rule dwindle.  

As far as who owns what?  My guess is the company "Artifact Entertainment" owns everything.  DA doesnt own Horizons, and who knows
if he even owns Demise at this point.  If these all became the company's intellectual property, then all DA owns is his shares in the 
company and the shirt on his back.  This makes alot of sense to me, again if I'm Mr. Deep Pockets, i want the company to own all the assets
and not just one person.

Just another thought, did anyone ever consider that maybe DA was "voted out" by the board (of which he's apart of)?  Lets say the 6 largest
shareholders in the company are the board of directors.  They got together to have a "no confidence" vote in the current project lead after which
DA was voted out and another voted in (or at least placed as "the interum" lead and subsequently radified by the board as the new lead.)  

Bottom line is, it was probably more than 2 people within the company who thought it a positive thing to get DA out of the project lead position... for the benefit of the company and the project.  All you DA fans should consider this possibility.  Geeze, If I wrote up a bunch of "design" documents listing what Way Cool(TM) features my MMORG was gonna have, would you all love me too?  What have I done to prove that I would ever
be able to deliver such a game?  One could look at my previous title as an example of whats the come... an unfinished, bug ridden, behind the times game, written in Visual Basic, and which only appealed to a small niche of old school hack 'n slash game players.  DA's track record subsequent to the games release, was one of a bunch of promises that
were never kept.  The multiplayer code was absymal and never got fixed (dont take my word for it, ask any of the Demise players... any of the ones that are left anyway).  Oh.. but DA is a better designer everyone says.. he doesnt have to be a great coder.  All the more reason that he shouldnt be put in charge I say.  The guys who founded Turbine were good C/C++ programmers with a good idea for a game.   They were able to work and get a somewhat playable demo out (which they used to shop to publishers) before they ever got funding.  I think they ended up reworking much/all of the code, but thats besides the point.  DA seemed to want to get funding so that he could hire a bunch of people and then create the game he envisioned.  Well gosh, wouldnt it be nice if we could all do that?  I'm sure everyone here has a great game idea and all they need is the money to hire the people who could write it for them.  Sounds great on paper doesnt it?  

I'll give DA credit though, he got alot further than most people.  He actually found some poor saps to front alot of money for a project that has an extremely slim chance of being successful.  And i'm talking about game development in general.  Most games that make it to market fail from an economic perspective.  But a MMORG just compounds the entire problem and worsens the odds of success.   

